# Page folder structure

Organize each app by **page ownership**. If a file is used by one page, it lives in that page folder. Promote to `shared/` only after a second page actually imports it.

```text
apps/<app>/src/pages/<PageName>/
  <PageName>.tsx
  index.ts
  component/
  constant/
  types/
  util/
  store/
```

## Folders and names

- Use singular folder names: `component/`, `constant/`, `types/`, `util/`, `store/`.
- Utility code belongs in `util/`, never `lib/`. `lib/` is reserved for Nx libraries under `libs/`.
- Create a folder only when it has files. Every folder that exists must have an `index.ts`.

## Import through the folder

Consumers import a folder, not a file inside it:

```ts
// ❌ BAD
import { CompanySettingsForm } from './component/CompanySettingsForm';
import { describeCompanyError } from './util/describe-company-error';

// ✅ GOOD
import { CompanySettingsForm } from './component';
import { describeCompanyError } from './util';
```

`index.ts` is the only file that imports sibling implementation files. Files in the same folder may import a sibling file when importing the barrel would cycle.

## Shared vs page-owned

- Page-only code stays under `pages/<PageName>/`.
- Cross-page code goes in `src/shared/{component,constant,types,util}`.
- App bootstrap/store helpers that no page owns go in `src/util/` or `src/store/`.
- `App.tsx` stays a route table (or a thin page mount). Screen markup belongs in the page file.
- API request/response types come from `libs/<mfe>/contract` (`@cms/<mfe>-contract`), not from page `types/`. See `.cursor/rules/mfe-lib-folder-structure.mdc`.
